\section{Limitations and honest scope}\label{sec:limits}
(1) Scope of the empirical win: on the CIFAR-10-C \emph{stress} grid \textsc{KGA} beats both
trivial policies for Tent and EATA at $0\%$ false-adapt (Table~\ref{tab:decisive}) and ties
the collapse-resistant SAR; on ImageNet-C (Table~\ref{tab:imagenetc}) the pattern \emph{flips}---
Tent and EATA are robust so \textsc{KGA} ties always-adapt, while SAR collapses and \textsc{KGA}
beats both. On \emph{per-corruption} CIFAR-10-C and within a single online stream (both
helpful-dominated) it again \emph{ties} the better fixed policy. The common rule across all of
these: \textsc{KGA} never underperforms the better trivial policy, and \emph{strictly} wins only
where catastrophic, detectable harm is actually present---and which method that is depends on the
benchmark. Larger-scale corroboration (CIFAR-100-C, ViT-B scale) and a tighter online certificate
are future work.
(2) The CIFAR-10-C corruptions are synthetic. The \emph{natural}-shift track
(WILDS Camelyon17, 4 seeds) is \emph{complete} (Table~\ref{tab:camelyon}) and is, candidly, a
\emph{knowably-helpful} regime: adaptation helps on every seed, so always-adapt is optimal and
\textsc{KGA}'s caution costs $\approx\!0.02$ regret---it beats always-freeze but not
always-adapt. The ImageNet-C / ResNet-50 track (Table~\ref{tab:imagenetc}) is now \emph{complete}
($36$ cells, $4000$ images/cell): it confirms a harmful regime at ImageNet scale, but only for
SAR (which collapses on $31\%$ of cells); Tent and EATA are robust here, so \textsc{KGA} ties
always-adapt rather than beating it. We flag one caveat for scrutiny: SAR's collapse in our runs
concentrates at the \emph{larger} batch size, which is atypical for entropy-stabilised TTA, so the
SAR row warrants re-checking against the reference implementation before the SAR win is treated as
load-bearing. Camelyon17 uses a $\sim\!94\%$ patch subset (storage-limited extraction). A second
natural-shift track (CIFAR-10.1, Table~\ref{tab:cifar101}) probes the opposite boundary---a
\emph{low-margin} real shift where most conditions are marginal: \textsc{KGA} abstains heavily,
never false-adapts, ties the better trivial policy for Tent/EATA, and narrowly beats both for SAR
(single-seed quick protocol). WILDS
iWildCam, ViT-B scale, and TTT/SHOT baselines remain future work.
(3) The conformal radius assumes task exchangeability.
(4) The sign-of-difference characterization is proved for binary, multiclass, and
regression (Theorems~\ref{thm:disagree}--\ref{thm:disagree-reg}); only the label-free
\emph{bracketing} of the ordinal comparison (Conjecture~\ref{conj:gen}) remains open.
(5) The non-identifiability witness is now both constructed and quantitative
(Proposition~\ref{thm:imp-quant}).
(6) \textsc{KGA}'s value scales with how often catastrophic, detectable harm
occurs---precisely the quantity the theory characterizes; in benign domains
always-adapt is strong and the certificate is mainly safety insurance.

